    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle fundamentally states that there is an inherent, unavoidable limit to the precision with which certain pairs of complementary physical properties of a particle can be known simultaneously. Specifically, the position ($\\Delta x$) and momentum ($\\Delta p$) of a particle cannot both be determined with perfect accuracy; this relationship is mathematically quantified as $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\frac{\\hbar}{2}$. This inequality signifies a trade-off: the more precisely you measure the position of a particle (making $\\Delta x$ smaller), the greater the inherent uncertainty introduced into its momentum, and vice versa. This is not a limitation of our measuring instruments, but a fundamental property of quantum mechanics itself.",
      "source": "target",
